  "read": {
    "headline": "Fresh records on a lower ten-year. Korea and crypto fell.",
    "standfirst": "Tuesday brought closing records for the S&P 500 and, for a second day, the Nasdaq Composite, on lower yields. The S&P 500 rose 0.58% to 7,818.93 and the Nasdaq Composite 0.45% to 27,599.79, and the Dow rose 0.49% to 51,521.28, its fourth straight gain; utilities led with a 3.0% rise and health care was the only sector lower. The ten-year closed at 5.267%, down about 4bp, even though the three-year auction went at 4.932%, the highest since 2006. Small caps fell, the Russell 2000 down 0.59%. The August trade deficit widened to $105.6 billion, the largest since March 2025, as imports hit a record $420.8 billion on AI-related goods and crude oil, and euro-area retail sales grew 0.8% against 1.0% expected. This morning Asia is lower: the Kospi is down about 1.5% with LG Electronics down 9%, the Nikkei about 0.5% on profit-taking in chip stocks, and India's central bank raised its rate by 25bp to 5.5%. Brent is $101.45 on Houthi attacks on Saudi Arabia and more tanker incidents near Hormuz. Today: the ten-year auction at 13:00 ET and the Fed minutes at 14:00 ET.",
    "points": [
      {
        "title": "Records on a 5.27% ten-year. Utilities rose 3%, and small caps fell.",
        "text": "The S&P 500 closed at 7,818.93, up 0.58%, and the Nasdaq Composite at 27,599.79, up 0.45%, both records; the Dow rose 0.49% to 51,521.28, its fourth straight gain, and the Russell 2000 fell 0.59% to 2,830.30. The VIX closed at 15.01. Utilities rose about 3.0%, the sector fund 2.98%, the biggest one-day gain among the equity funds, with consumer discretionary up 1.39% in the index and real estate 1.13%; health care, down 0.16%, was the only sector to fall. Software and services rose 1.3% to a level not seen since November 2025 as earnings forecasts were revised higher, Lamb Weston rose about 12% on results and a raised outlook, and Nvidia is nearing a $6 trillion value. Over a month technology is up 7.86% and every other sector fund is lower, utilities down 4.46%; over three months utilities are still 9.26% lower. The small-cap funds fell 0.67% to 1.45%. Futures were little changed overnight."
      },
      {
        "title": "Bond yields fell for a day. The three-year auction was the highest since 2006.",
        "text": "The ten-year closed at 5.267%, down about 4bp from 5.31%, and the two-year at 4.80%; the long Treasury fund rose 0.22% and the 7–10 year fund 0.23%, and it is still down 6.00% and 3.39% over a month. The help came from oil, which fell to a one-month low in WTI early on Tuesday, and from a stock market that looked past the bond market. The $58 billion three-year auction went at 4.932%, the highest since May 2006, with a bid-to-cover of 2.62 against a 2.64 ten-auction average and indirect bidders at 57.6% against a 63% average; it cleared slightly through the when-issued yield, and Reuters called the demand solid. San Francisco's Daly said inflation may last longer than expected and that more hikes may be needed; Kansas City's Schmid said inflation is 'frustrating' and called AI one of its largest drivers. October's hike is priced at about 19–20%, from about 51% a week ago, with December about 85%. Ten-year notes were back near 5.30–5.31% in Asia this morning."
      },
      {
        "title": "A $105.6 billion trade deficit. Imports hit a record on AI goods and oil.",
        "text": "The August deficit was $105.6 billion against $102.1 billion expected, the widest since March 2025, with July revised to $92.8 billion from $88.6 billion. Imports rose 4.3% to a record $420.8 billion, led by crude oil, capital goods such as semiconductors, and nonmonetary gold; exports rose 1.4% to $315.2 billion. Capital Economics said third-quarter GDP growth would come in well below its 4.0% forecast, closer to 2.5%, while Nationwide's Oren Klachkin called it a sign of strong domestic demand rather than weakness. The year-to-date deficit of $138.2 billion is nearly 20% below a year ago, according to CNBC. The Atlanta Fed's GDPNow had net exports subtracting 2.59 points from growth before the release. Today the calendar is thin until the afternoon: MBA applications, the EIA's oil stocks at 10:30 ET, the ten-year auction and the minutes."
      },
      {
        "title": "Europe closed higher on French relief. Euro retail sales and German orders disappointed.",
        "text": "The Stoxx 600 rose 0.48% to 636.63, the FTSE 100 0.42% to 10,541.69, the CAC 40 0.40% to 7,865.07 and the DAX 0.77% to 25,449.19, with Genmab up 7.7% on a lymphoma trial; French, Italian and Greek bonds rallied after the leading candidate in next spring's presidential election outlined plans to cut spending, and the euro rebounded 0.34% from Monday's 16-month low. Against that, euro-area retail sales grew 0.8% year on year against 1.0% expected, with volumes up 0.1% on the month against 0.2%, and German factory orders fell 10.6% in August, the most in seven months. The euro is $1.12 this morning, down 3.38% over a month, with futures pointing to lower opens, the CAC about 0.7% and the DAX 0.5%. One estimate has the market pricing about two and a half more ECB hikes, from three and a half at the end of September, after inflation printed 3.8%; the bund yield fell 1.5bp to 3.478% and the gilt 4.3bp to 5.375%. The ECB's accounts are out on Thursday."
      },
      {
        "title": "Asia fell. Korea down 1.5%, LG Electronics down 9%, and India raised rates.",
        "text": "The Kospi is down 1.55% at 6,833.90 as LG Electronics fell about 9% after preliminary third-quarter profit missed estimates, and the Korea ETF fell 2.64% in New York; Taiwan is down 0.32%, Hong Kong down 0.56% with its biotech index down about 4%, and Australia up 0.52%. The Nikkei is down about 0.5% at 70,331 after rising nearly 2,400 yen in two sessions, with chip equipment and memory stocks sold for profit, while the Topix touched a record; the yen weakened to 158.4 and Japan's real cash earnings rose 1.5% in August, an eighth straight rise. India's central bank raised its repo rate by 25bp to 5.5%, its first hike in about three and a half years, and the rupee is 96.55 per dollar, about 2% weaker over a month; the Sensex is down 0.6% and the India ETF is down 6.37% over a month. Mainland China's markets are shut until 8 October, so the Asia moves are a thin read."
      },
      {
        "title": "Oil is $101 again. Houthis hit Saudi airports. Crypto fell on all nine.",
        "text": "December Brent is $101.45, up 0.86%, and WTI $90.09, up 0.73%: Yemen's Houthis targeted Saudi airports at Jazan and Najran and claimed an attack on a major refinery, the UK's maritime agency has logged nine tanker incidents near Hormuz this month, and a storm is heading for the Gulf of Mexico. Limits came from Middle East exports other than Iran's returning toward pre-war levels and the G7's emergency stock release; the EIA raised its fourth-quarter Brent forecast to about $105, $14 above its last. Brent's 1M column, +3.60% against WTI's −2.82%, still straddles the 30 September roll to December. Gold fell 0.65% to $4,160 after touching about $4,100, a two-month low, on Tuesday. All nine coins fell: Bitcoin 1.56% to $84,208, Ether 2.99% and Cardano 4.14%, and about $404 million of leveraged long positions were liquidated in an hour early Wednesday as Bitcoin slid to about $83,800. Brazil's Ibovespa gave back 0.52% to 205,835 after Monday's 7.7% surge, the real near 4.98."
      }
    ]
  },
